# Guide scenarios: worked examples of the scenario language, put here by Bridge Workbench. They are
# yours to edit or delete; Settings > Samples > restore puts the file back only if it is missing.
#
# HIDDEN UNTIL ASKED FOR. Every scenario here is tagged `guide`, and the deals list leaves a guide out
# until you pick the `guide` group (the groups button, or ctrl+g). Most are in the system `2over1`, so
# set the list's system to 2over1 (or every system) to see them.
#
# Each one shows one thing the language can say, using 2/1 game forcing meanings. Where a built-in
# scenario covers the same auction, the comment names it: the built-ins are the Swedish Club played by
# this program's author, and the difference in meaning is usually the point. A guide has a name of its
# own (guide-...) because a built-in scenario always wins a name it shares with a file.
#
# A scenario is a name, a description, and lines that AND together:
#
#   quantities   hcp  controls  spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs  longest  seat
#   comparisons  <  <=  =  !=  >=  >        or a range:  hcp in 15..17
#   against each other, and added up:       spades > hearts    spades + hearts >= 9
#   other atoms  balanced   holds(spades, ace)   vulnerable  favourable  unfavourable
#   operators    and  or  not  ( )


# 0. A SYSTEM AND ITS MEANINGS.
# A meaning is a condition with a name, written once in a system and used by name in its scenarios
# (`north: nt-opening`). A meaning may use others (`major-opening` below).
#
# A MEANING'S ALTERNATIVES are the hands some players count and others do not, each an `also` line under it.
# A deal kept only through one carries its name on the board, so the debatable hands are marked as such.
#
# YOUR OWN VERSION OF A SYSTEM extends it and changes only what you play differently. In a file of your
# own (not this one, so a restore never touches it), write for instance:
#
#     system ours extends 2over1 "2/1 as we play it"
#       meaning nt-opening
#         without "4441, singleton honour"
#       meaning major-opening: spade-opening or heart-opening or hcp = 11 and longest >= 6 and (spades >= 6 or hearts >= 6)
#
# A meaning named with NO condition keeps the inherited one and switches its alternatives: `without` turns one
# off, `also` adds your own. A meaning WITH a condition replaces it whole. Then set the deals list's system to
# `ours`: every 2over1 scenario below is listed under it and reads your meanings. The scenario editor's check
# says where each meaning came from ("nt-opening from 2over1, amended by ours").
system 2over1 "2/1 game forcing: a 15-17 notrump, five-card majors, a strong 2C"
  # A 2/1 notrump is 15-17; many players also open some hands that are not quite that.
  meaning nt-opening: hcp in 15..17 and balanced
    also "14, five-card suit": hcp = 14 and balanced and longest = 5
    also "4441, singleton honour": hcp in 15..17 and longest = 4 and ((spades = 1 and (holds(spades, ace) or holds(spades, king))) or (hearts = 1 and (holds(hearts, ace) or holds(hearts, king))) or (diamonds = 1 and (holds(diamonds, ace) or holds(diamonds, king))) or (clubs = 1 and (holds(clubs, ace) or holds(clubs, king))))
  # The longer major first, the higher with five of each: one suit's length against another's.
  meaning spade-opening: spades >= 5 and spades >= hearts and hcp in 12..21
  meaning heart-opening: hearts >= 5 and hearts > spades and hcp in 12..21
  meaning major-opening: spade-opening or heart-opening
  meaning strong-2c: hcp >= 22


# 1. THE BASIC SHAPE, through a MEANING WITH ALTERNATIVES.
# The built-in `1n` is a 16-18 notrump. This one uses the system's `nt-opening`, 15-17 plus the two kinds of
# hand above that some players open 1NT anyway; a deal only one of them lets in shows its name on the board, so
# you can see which hands are the debatable ones. Press check in the scenario editor to see how many deals each
# one adds. Every scenario that opens 1NT gets the same alternatives, from the one meaning.
scenario guide-1nt "2/1: North opens 1NT, 15-17 - and the hands some open 1NT anyway"
  system: 2over1
  tags: guide
  north: nt-opening


# 2. A MEANING THAT USES OTHERS, and a SCENARIO'S OWN alternative.
# The built-in `1hs` is a limited 11-15 opening (a stronger hand opens 1C). A 2/1 major is 12-21 with a
# five-card suit, and `major-opening` is the system's two major openings together. (No notrump hand is
# caught here: `balanced` never includes a five-card major.) An `also` line in a SCENARIO names one more kind
# of hand for that scenario alone: it REPLACES the north lines above it, here a light opening with a six-card
# suit.
scenario guide-1m "2/1: North opens one of a major, 12-21"
  system: 2over1
  tags: guide
  north: major-opening
  also "11 with a six-card suit" north: (spades >= 6 or hearts >= 6) and hcp = 11


# 3. TWO HANDS: one line for each seat.
# 1S - 1NT is semi-forcing in 2/1: 6-12 points without three spades. In Acol the same 1NT is 6-9 - a
# system extending this one would override a meaning for it, not copy the scenario.
scenario guide-1s-1nt "2/1: 1S - 1NT, semi-forcing (6-12, fewer than three spades)"
  system: 2over1
  tags: guide
  north: spade-opening
  south: hcp in 6..12 and spades <= 2


# 4. THE 2/1 RESPONSE ITSELF, with OR inside a line.
# A new suit at the two level forces to game. The built-in `1hs_game-force` is any 13+ response to a
# limited major; this is the response that gives the system its name.
scenario guide-1s-2over1 "2/1: 1S - 2C, 2D or 2H, game forcing"
  system: 2over1
  tags: guide
  north: spade-opening
  south: hcp >= 12 and spades <= 2 and (clubs >= 4 or diamonds >= 4 or hearts >= 5)


# 5. AN ALTERNATIVE ON THE SECOND HAND.
# Jacoby 2NT: a game-forcing raise with four trumps. Some partnerships make it with a good 12 and a
# shortage; spelling "a singleton or void somewhere" is three ORs, since there is no word for shortest.
scenario guide-jacoby-2nt "2/1: 1H - 2NT, Jacoby (four hearts, game forcing)"
  system: 2over1
  tags: guide
  north: heart-opening
  south: hearts >= 4 and hcp >= 13
  also "12 with a shortage" south: hearts >= 4 and hcp = 12 and (spades <= 1 or diamonds <= 1 or clubs <= 1)


# 6. SEAT AND VULNERABILITY.
# A weak two depends on where it is bid from. `seat` is the hand's place in the auction (1 = dealer);
# `favourable` means not vulnerable against vulnerable opponents. Deals are dealt with random dealers
# and vulnerability, and each is checked at the table it is shown at. The built-in `2hs-weak` ignores
# both.
scenario guide-weak-2h "2/1: a weak two in hearts - sound in first and second seat, lighter later"
  system: 2over1
  tags: guide, preempt
  north: seat <= 2 and hearts = 6 and hcp in 5..10 and spades <= 3
  also "third seat, five cards" north: seat = 3 and hearts = 5 and hcp in 6..10 and spades <= 3 and (holds(hearts, ace) or holds(hearts, king))
  also "favourable, light" north: seat <= 3 and favourable and hearts = 6 and hcp in 3..10 and spades <= 3


# 7. THE SAME NAME, A DIFFERENT CALL.
# In 2/1, 2C is the strong artificial opening. The built-in `2c` is a club one-suiter of 11-15 - which
# is exactly why a scenario says whose meanings it uses. "Nine playing tricks" cannot be counted in this
# language; a long suit with 19-21 is a fair stand-in, and names itself on the board.
scenario guide-2c-strong "2/1: North opens a strong, artificial 2C"
  system: 2over1
  tags: guide
  north: strong-2c
  also "19-21, a long suit" north: hcp in 19..21 and longest >= 7


# 8. A PARTNERSHIP LINE.
# north-south: reads both hands together, so hcp is the side's total. The built-in `1n_slam-try` asks for
# 29+ opposite a 16-18 notrump; 33 together is the old rule for 6NT, whatever the notrump range.
scenario guide-1nt-small-slam "2/1: 1NT opener, and 33+ between North and South"
  system: 2over1
  tags: guide, slam
  north: nt-opening
  north-south: hcp >= 33


# 9. NO SYSTEM, AND A FIXED DEALER.
# The opponents' bidding is the same whatever you play, so this one has no system line and is listed
# under every system - and, having no system, it cannot use a system's meanings. `dealer: east` makes
# East deal every board, so East's 1NT is the first call. The built-in `opp-1n-15-17` is the same idea.
scenario guide-opp-1nt "East opens a 15-17 1NT; South has a five-card major to show"
  tags: guide, competitive
  dealer: east
  east: hcp in 15..17 and balanced
  south: hcp in 8..15 and (spades >= 5 or hearts >= 5)
